Finish every decode before vaEndPicture returns, for nvidia-vaapi-driver v0.0.18
(applied after linear-surfaces.patch).

Upstream, vaEndPicture only queues the decoded picture for the resolve thread,
which later copies it into the surface's exported buffer; only vaSyncSurface
waits for that copy. Chromium shows a decoded surface by importing that dma-buf
and relies on implicit fences instead of vaSyncSurface, and CUDA attaches none.
So it could show a surface before its new picture was copied in, getting
whatever the surface held last: a frame a few back in its pool. Viewers saw
screen shares jump back 3-4 frames, over and over (reported 2026-11-19 on a
GTX 1650 under X11, H.264; gone with hardware decode off).

- vaEndPicture waits for the resolve (NVD_ASYNC_RESOLVE=1 restores upstream).
- The resolve thread synchronises its CUDA stream before marking the surface
  resolved: a device-to-device cuMemcpy2D does not wait on the host.

diff -ruN --text a/src/direct/direct-export-buf.c b/src/direct/direct-export-buf.c
--- a/src/direct/direct-export-buf.c	2026-11-19 17:17:31.812297500 -0500
+++ b/src/direct/direct-export-buf.c	2026-11-19 17:17:48.490323400 -0500
@@ -907,6 +907,14 @@
 
     free(stagingPlane);
 
+    // A device-to-device cuMemcpy2D does not wait for the copy on the host, so
+    // the frame may still be landing in the exported buffer. Clients that
+    // import the dma-buf (Chromium) get no fence from CUDA to order against:
+    // wait here, so "resolved" means the picture is really in the buffer.
+    if (CHECK_CUDA_RESULT(drv->cu->cuStreamSynchronize(0))) {
+        return false;
+    }
+
     //notify anyone waiting for us to be resolved
     pthread_mutex_lock(&surface->mutex);
     surface->resolving = 0;
diff -ruN --text a/src/vabackend.c b/src/vabackend.c
--- a/src/vabackend.c	2026-11-19 17:17:31.837296000 -0500
+++ b/src/vabackend.c	2026-11-19 17:17:48.498836700 -0500
@@ -3024,6 +3024,15 @@
     return VA_STATUS_SUCCESS;
 }
 
+static bool asyncResolveAllowed(void) {
+    static int allowed = -1;
+    if (allowed < 0) {
+        const char *v = getenv("NVD_ASYNC_RESOLVE");
+        allowed = v != NULL && v[0] == '1';
+    }
+    return allowed == 1;
+}
+
 static VAStatus nvEndPicture(
         VADriverContextP ctx,
         VAContextID context
@@ -3082,6 +3091,17 @@
     //Wake up the resolve thread
     pthread_cond_signal(&nvCtx->resolveCondition);
 
+    // Chromium shows a decoded surface by importing its dma-buf and relies on
+    // implicit fences, never calling vaSyncSurface; CUDA sets none. Without
+    // this it could show a surface before the resolve thread copied the new
+    // picture in, i.e. whatever that surface last held: a frame a few back in
+    // its pool, so the video jumped back and forth. Finish the resolve before
+    // returning (a few ms of decode latency). NVD_ASYNC_RESOLVE=1 restores
+    // upstream behaviour.
+    if (!asyncResolveAllowed()) {
+        waitSurfaceResolved(surface);
+    }
+
     return status;
 }
 
